ties and differences in industrial relations within each region, and in particular the impact of the design of each national industrial relations system on outcomes for workers, employers, and national governments.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 360356, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "2350", "titleShort": "Work,Labor&Capital in Global", "titleLong": "Work, Labor, and Capital in the Global Economy", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 10667, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:40AM", "timeEnd": "12:55PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 17, "netid": "rb41", "firstName": "Rosemary", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Batt"}, {"instrAssignSeq": 18, "netid": "vld7", "firstName": "Virginia", "middleName": "L", "lastName": "Doellgast"}, {"instrAssignSeq": 19, "netid": "edf48", "firstName": "Eli", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Friedman"}, {"instrAssignSeq": 20, "netid": "jj729", "firstName": "Jason", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Judd"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 115", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS115", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Provides an introduction to how globalization is changing the nature of work, labor, and capital. It examines both contemporary and historical debates about globalization, but also covers a number of interrelated issues, including the regulation of labor standards, the mobility of capital, the rise of global production systems, international labor, and responses to globalization. Lectures and discussion for the topics mentioned above will be grounded in the experiences of different countries, firms, workplaces, industrial sectors, and individuals.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(CU-ITL, EC-SAP)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall, Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: first-year and sophomore students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 370736, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "2390", "titleShort": "People Power: Resistance, Prot", "titleLong": "People Power: Resistance, Protest and Revolution", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 10995, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "08:40AM", "timeEnd": "09:55AM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "db833", "firstName": "Dina", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Bishara"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 112", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS112", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "The Arab uprisings of 2010/2011\u00a0brought renewed attention to the power of ordinary citizens to collectively\u00a0overthrow their governments. This course introduces\u00a0theories of collective action,\u00a0resistance, protest,\u00a0and revolutions. It addresses the following\u00a0questions:\u00a0How do aggrieved citizens make claims against their governments, especially in non-democratic contexts? When and why\u00a0do people act collectively to make those claims? How do governments respond to various forms of protest? What constitutes a revolution?\u00a0Why do some succeed and others fail? We pay particular attention to different forms of workers' mobilization and to the role of labor and other social classes in revolutions.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "Fulfills the ILR Sophomore Writing Requirement.", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: sophomores or, others with permission who have not satisfied their ILR Sophomore Writing Requirement.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 370745, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "4337", "titleShort": "Labor and Employment in the Mi", "titleLong": "Labor and Employment in the Middle East and North Africa", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 10997, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:40AM", "timeEnd": "12:55PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "db833", "firstName": "Dina", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Bishara"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 112", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS112", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "6337", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NES", "catalogNbr": "4337", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NES", "catalogNbr": "6337", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This course introduces students to the history, evolution and trajectory of state-labor relations, labor activism, and the politics of unemployment in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA). As the region with the highest concentration of non-democratic regimes in the world, the MENA provides a rich context for examining state efforts to control interest representation, and workers' struggles for freedom of association. In addition, the region features diverse political economic systems, making it ideal for examining the interaction between resource endowments and labor market dynamics. Finally, the region is ripe for the study of youth activism and the mobilization of the unemployed given that youth unemployment rates are higher in the MENA than any other world region.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 364423, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "4340", "titleShort": "Special Topics in ICL", "titleLong": "Special Topics in International and Comparative Labor", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 18930, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "03/12/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "vm348", "firstName": "Vincenzo", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Maccarrone"}], "pattern": "MW", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 215", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS215", "meetingTopicDescription": null}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "This course offers a critical perspective on European labor relations. Europe is often compared positively to the US in terms of the residual strength of its industrial relations institutions and employment protection legislation. However, over the last decades European industrial relations have undergone significant changes, at the national, sectoral and supranational level. Over seven weeks, the course offers an overview and an assessment of such changes."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "03/20/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "Changing Industrial Relations in Europe", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 1.5, "unitsMaximum": 1.5, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "7W1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "03/20/2024", "sessionLong": "Seven Week - First", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Devoted to new topics in the field. The specific content and emphasis vary depending upon the interests of the faculty member teaching the course.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(CU-ITL)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall, Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 373482, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "4352", "titleShort": "Healthcare and Financializtn", "titleLong": "From Social Goods to Private Gain: Healthcare in the Age of Financialization", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 17716, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:00PM", "timeEnd": "04:30PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "rb41", "firstName": "Rosemary", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Batt"}], "pattern": "M", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 109", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS109", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Financialization is a process in which financial actors increasingly dominate the US economy. While it is occurring across most industries, it has penetrated the US healthcare sector at an alarming rate. Historically, public and non-profit healthcare providers had a patient care mission, but increasingly, large for-profit corporations and Wall Street investors are dominant players. How do these changes affect the quality of jobs, job security, patient care, and equal access to care? This course examines these issues from a multi-stakeholder perspective. It is interdisciplinary \u2013 drawing on readings, cases, and guest speakers from political science, sociology, industrial relations, health economics, and public health. It examines the intersection between regulatory policies, financial actors, and healthcare outcomes. Part I considers macro-level changes in regulatory policies that have facilitated change. Part II focuses on the dynamics of conflict and change in particular healthcare arenas, including hospitals, nursing homes, mental health, big pharma, HealthIT, and others. Part III examines the evidence on outcomes for healthcare workers, patients, and providers -- and how labor and patient advocacy groups have responded. This provides the basis for class debates on organizational and public policy options for reform.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 373606, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "4377", "titleShort": "Issues in South Asian Studies", "titleLong": "Issues in South Asian Studies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 18763, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "12:15PM", "timeEnd": "01:30PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "sb2626", "firstName": "Sarah", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Besky"}], "pattern": "M", "facilityDescr": "Uris Hall G08", "bldgDescr": "Uris Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "URHG08", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Incorrect course description currently displayed. Please see attached syllabus for correct description."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 1.5, "unitsMaximum": 1.5, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "6377", "type": "W"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This is an events-based course. Students will attend ten seminars in the South Asia Program seminar series. The work of scholars, filmmakers, and artists presenting research in the series spans the region and its diasporas (e.g., India, Pakistan, Nepal, Bangladesh, Afghanistan, Bhutan, and Indian Ocean worlds). Topics considered will cross the humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences. Students will attend these events and engage with the material presented in short response papers and supplemental readings. The objective of this course is to offer students, whether they are familiar with the region or not, new perspectives on the lived experiences of South Asia. Students will also become familiar with interdisciplinary area studies as an intellectual project.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall, Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 371383, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "4940", "titleShort": "ILR Global Scholars Capstone", "titleLong": "ILR Global Scholars Capstone", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 10937, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "03:35PM", "timeEnd": "04:25PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "edf48", "firstName": "Eli", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Friedman"}], "pattern": "M", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 107", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS107", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 1, "unitsMaximum": 1, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "SUI", "gradingBasisShort": "S/U NoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Satisfactory/Unsatisfactory (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This capstone course is open to ILR Global Scholars (GS) during their last semester of their senior year. The course is designed for GS to incorporate their education and experience abroad with the concepts and theories they have gained while at the ILR School. The overarching goal of this class is to bring GS together to collectively reflect on and identify key aspects of intercultural competency related to their personal and cultural growth, academic and professional development and roles as future global citizens. The structure of the course will be group discussions, peer review, and individual reflection papers. The final capstone reflection paper will fulfill the course requirement and the Global Scholars Program final reflection requirement.\u00a0", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: students participating in the ILR Global Scholars program.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 370275, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "5310", "titleShort": "Labor Solidarity and Union Cam", "titleLong": "Labor Solidarity and Union Campaigns", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 17717, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "01:25PM", "timeEnd": "02:40PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "vld7", "firstName": "Virginia", "middleName": "L", "lastName": "Doellgast"}], "pattern": "MW", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 111", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS111", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "In this class, we will compare case studies of labor union and other worker-led campaigns based on old and new forms of solidarity, both within countries and transnationally. Each week, we will discuss a different case study through presentations by labor and community organizers and researchers. Students will produce a website with case descriptions, interview transcripts, and other resources.\u202fPast case studies include the Volkswagen and Bank Workers campaigns in the US, international organizing at\u00a0RyanAir\u00a0and Amazon in Europe, migrant worker organizing at the Shelter for Foreign workers initiative in South Korea, and\u202fcollective action\u202fby app-based workers\u202fin Argentina.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "GR", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 370746, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "6337", "titleShort": "Labor and Employment in the Mi", "titleLong": "Labor and Employment in the Middle East and North Africa", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 10998, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:40AM", "timeEnd": "12:55PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "db833", "firstName": "Dina", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Bishara"}], "pattern": "TR", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall 112", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "IVS112", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "4337", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NES", "catalogNbr": "4337", "type": "B"}, {"subject": "NES", "catalogNbr": "6337", "type": "B"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This course introduces students to the history, evolution and trajectory of state-labor relations, labor activism, and the politics of unemployment in the Middle East and North Africa (MENA). As the region with the highest concentration of non-democratic regimes in the world, the MENA provides a rich context for examining state efforts to control interest representation, and workers' struggles for freedom of association. In addition, the region features diverse political economic systems, making it ideal for examining the interaction between resource endowments and labor market dynamics. Finally, the region is ripe for the study of youth activism and the mobilization of the unemployed given that youth unemployment rates are higher in the MENA than any other world region.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "GR", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 373607, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "6377", "titleShort": "Issues in South Asian Studies", "titleLong": "Issues in South Asian Studies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 18764, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "12:15PM", "timeEnd": "01:30PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "sb2626", "firstName": "Sarah", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Besky"}], "pattern": "M", "facilityDescr": "Uris Hall G08", "bldgDescr": "Uris Hall", "facilityDescrshort": "URHG08", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 1.5, "unitsMaximum": 1.5, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "4377", "type": "W"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This is an events-based course. Students will attend ten seminars in the South Asia Program seminar series. The work of scholars, filmmakers, and artists presenting research in the series spans the region and its diasporas (e.g., India, Pakistan, Nepal, Bangladesh, Afghanistan, Bhutan, and Indian Ocean worlds). Topics considered will cross the humanities, social sciences, and natural sciences. Students will attend these events and engage with the material presented in short response papers and supplemental readings. The objective of this course is to offer students, whether they are familiar with the region or not, new perspectives on the lived experiences of South Asia. Students will also become familiar with interdisciplinary area studies as an intellectual project.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall, Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "GR", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2867, "crseId": 365764, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "ILRIC", "catalogNbr": "7350", "titleShort": "Labor Sociology", "titleLong": "Labor Sociology", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 18211, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:00PM", "timeEnd": "04:30PM", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/07/2024", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "edf48", "firstName": "Eli", "middleName": "", "lastName": "Friedman"}], "pattern": "W", "facilityDescr": "Ives Hall Faculty Wing 381", "bldgDescr": "Ives Hall Faculty Wing", "facilityDescrshort": "IVF381", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/22/2024", "endDt": "05/18/2024", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "GRI", "gradingBasisShort": "GradeNoAud", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only (no audit)", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "GDEV", "catalogNbr": "7350", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "SOC", "catalogNbr": "7350", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/22/2024", "sessionEndDt": "05/18/2024", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "The object of our inquiry is labor: as an activity, a class position, a social movement, an institution, a political subject. A sociology of labor must take as its charge identifying the historically specific constitution of labor in relationship to processes of capitalist development, colonialism, gendered and racialized forms of domination, culture, and technology. Integrating an account of such broader social phenomena reveals insights into labor that would not be possible were we to restrict our inquiry to the workplace. But as one of the fundamental social categories, the concrete manifestation of multifarious labor in any given setting is simultaneously a window onto the basic architecture of society.", "catalogBreadth": "", "catalogDistr": "", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "Enrollment limited to: graduate students.", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "GR", "acadGroup": "IL", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}]}, "message": null, "meta": {"copyright": "Cornell University, Office of the University Registrar", "rosterDttm": "2024-05-19T19:32:44-0400", "catalogDttm": "2024-05-19T19:07:08-0400"}}